<psalm
	errorLevel="6"
	resolveFromConfigFile="true"
	findUnusedBaselineEntry="false"
	findUnusedCode="false"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xmlns="https://getpsalm.org/schema/config"
	xsi:schemaLocation="https://getpsalm.org/schema/config vendor/vimeo/psalm/config.xsd"
	errorBaseline="tests/psalm-baseline.xml">
	<projectFiles>
		<directory name="lib" />
		<!--
			THE STEP DEFINITIONS ARE ANALYSED TOO, and they were not before.

			They are ordinary PHP that only ever runs inside a live integration
			environment, so a mistake in them costs a full CI cycle — a Nextcloud
			and a Grafana boot — to discover. That is not hypothetical: a call to
			`theFileWasCreatedWhenItsDashboardWasCreated()` (the real method is
			`…WasCreatedWhenItsDashboardWas`; the name was written from the STEP
			TEXT rather than from the method) got all the way to a red integration
			leg, and nothing on the way could have caught it.

			`bin/check-step-definitions.sh` answers a different question — does the
			step TEXT resolve to a definition — and answered it correctly. Whether
			the PHP inside those definitions is coherent is Psalm's job, and until
			now nobody was asking.
		-->
		<directory name="tests/integration/bootstrap" />
		<ignoreFiles>
			<directory name="vendor" />
		</ignoreFiles>
	</projectFiles>
	<!--
		Load the nextcloud/ocp class definitions so Psalm resolves OCP\* symbols
		(IConfig, Files\Node, AppFramework\Controller, the Settings interfaces, …).
		Without this, Psalm reports every OCP reference as UndefinedClass /
		MissingDependency / UndefinedDocblockClass — ~176 false positives. We scan
		only the OCP package (not all of vendor) to keep analysis fast.
	-->
	<extraFiles>
		<directory name="vendor/nextcloud/ocp" />
	</extraFiles>
	<!--
		Hand-written stubs for the Server-app classes that live in a real Nextcloud
		but ship in neither nextcloud/ocp nor Composer: the Sabre WebDAV server
		(Sabre\DAV\*), the DAV connector's File node, and OCA\DAV\Events\SabrePluginAddEvent
		— all reached by the link-write guard plugin + its registration listener. Mirrors
		the n8n master's psalm.xml, which stubs the same DAV surface the same way.
	-->
	<stubs>
		<file name="tests/external-stubs.php" />
	</stubs>
	<issueHandlers>
		<!--
			PHP 8.2 added constants in traits, and composer.json declares ^8.1 —
			which is right for SHIPPED code, because Nextcloud 31 runs on 8.1.
			These three are in the integration step definitions, which ship to
			nobody and only ever run in CI on 8.4. Suppressed there and nowhere
			else, so the same construct in lib/ would still be an error.
		-->
		<ConstantDeclarationInTrait>
			<errorLevel type="suppress">
				<directory name="tests/integration" />
			</errorLevel>
		</ConstantDeclarationInTrait>
		<!--
			These reference classes that exist at runtime in a real Nextcloud but are
			NOT shipped in nextcloud/ocp, so Psalm can't see them: the private OC
			internals the pull's mimetype repair-steps + Team Folder actor-FS setup
			reach for (`OC::$SERVERROOT`/`OC::$configDir`, `OC_Util::setupFS`). Not real
			bugs, and there's no public stub to import. Mirrors the n8n master's
			psalm.xml (which pulls the same private symbols) and integration_openai.
		-->
		<UndefinedClass>
			<errorLevel type="suppress">
				<referencedClass name="OC" />
				<referencedClass name="OC_Util" />
				<referencedClass name="OC_User" />
				<referencedClass name="OCA\Files_Trashbin\Trash\ITrashItem" />
				<referencedClass name="OCA\Files_Trashbin\Trash\ITrashManager" />
			</errorLevel>
		</UndefinedClass>
		<!--
			The trash app is REMOVABLE, which is the whole reason {@see OCA\GrafanaSync\Service\TrashControl}
			resolves its manager lazily instead of taking it in the constructor — a hard
			dependency would make this app fail to boot on an instance without it. Psalm
			cannot see either interface for the same reason, so the suppression above is
			the honest counterpart of the runtime `interface_exists()` check.

			NO `UndefinedDocblockClass` BLOCK, though the n8n master carries one: psalm
			treats an unused suppression as an error in its own right, and here the two
			interfaces are only ever named in real type positions, never in a docblock.
			The master's TrashControl does name them in docblocks, which is the whole
			difference — a suppression copied across for symmetry fails the build.
		-->
		<!--
			MissingDependency doesn't support <referencedClass> in Psalm 6's schema.
			The only instances are IRootFolder reporting its base oc\hooks\emitter as
			missing — purely an OCP-stub artifact, not our code — so suppress the whole
			(low-value) issue type. Same as the master.
		-->
		<MissingDependency errorLevel="suppress" />
	</issueHandlers>
</psalm>
